1
00:00:00,913 --> 00:00:03,846
So now to come back to that question, does

2
00:00:04,022 --> 00:00:05,608
it run on my machine?

3
00:00:06,185 --> 00:00:08,910
There are 2 important parts to that answer.

4
00:00:09,470 --> 00:00:12,659
For one, as I explained before, preferably

5
00:00:12,852 --> 00:00:15,528
you have a GPU that can run the model

6
00:00:16,121 --> 00:00:18,365
and VRAM into which the parameters, the

7
00:00:18,365 --> 00:00:20,880
quantized parameters can be loaded. But if

8
00:00:20,880 --> 00:00:24,214
you don't, that's okay too. Your CPU and

9
00:00:24,230 --> 00:00:27,387
your regular system RAM can also handle

10
00:00:27,499 --> 00:00:29,566
these models. You will be able to run them

11
00:00:29,662 --> 00:00:32,564
locally without a GPU too. It will just be

12
00:00:32,564 --> 00:00:32,917
slower.

13
00:00:33,511 --> 00:00:34,891
Inference will be slower.

14
00:00:35,694 --> 00:00:38,069
But of course, the amount of memory you

15
00:00:38,085 --> 00:00:40,219
have, no matter if it's VRAM, which is

16
00:00:40,267 --> 00:00:42,643
preferable, or regular system memory,

17
00:00:43,300 --> 00:00:44,007
also matters.

18
00:00:44,986 --> 00:00:48,340
Because not all open models can be run on

19
00:00:48,596 --> 00:00:51,838
every laptop, on every machine, even with

20
00:00:51,886 --> 00:00:52,785
quantization.

21
00:00:53,732 --> 00:00:56,283
And the great thing about Hugging Face is

22
00:00:56,460 --> 00:00:59,123
that if you have an account, which you don't

23
00:00:59,123 --> 00:01:01,176
need for this course, but which you can

24
00:01:01,272 --> 00:01:02,090
create for free.

25
00:01:02,731 --> 00:01:03,565
If you have one,

26
00:01:04,318 --> 00:01:07,605
then under Settings and there Local Apps and

27
00:01:07,701 --> 00:01:08,214
Hardware,

28
00:01:08,888 --> 00:01:10,363
you can basically share

29
00:01:10,892 --> 00:01:12,704
your hardware profile.

30
00:01:13,233 --> 00:01:13,842
So in my case,

31
00:01:14,420 --> 00:01:18,428
I have an M1 Max MacBook Pro, so I added

32
00:01:18,428 --> 00:01:19,999
this here as my hardware.

33
00:01:21,250 --> 00:01:23,367
And it then tells me whether that's good or

34
00:01:23,495 --> 00:01:26,333
bad, and you see I'm on the lower end here.

35
00:01:27,264 --> 00:01:30,041
But the great thing is that I got 64

36
00:01:30,138 --> 00:01:32,851
gigabytes of so-called unified memory, which

37
00:01:32,899 --> 00:01:35,661
is a combination of system memory and VRAM.

38
00:01:35,998 --> 00:01:38,391
So that's pretty good and allows me to load

39
00:01:38,423 --> 00:01:40,398
quite big models into memory.

40
00:01:40,992 --> 00:01:43,609
But the memory is definitely not as fast as

41
00:01:43,706 --> 00:01:47,158
on high-end NVIDIA GPUs, for example. It's

42
00:01:47,158 --> 00:01:48,587
always a trade-off in the end.

43
00:01:49,325 --> 00:01:51,621
But since I added this hardware profile

44
00:01:51,734 --> 00:01:55,325
here, on the model pages of these quantized

45
00:01:55,438 --> 00:01:55,806
models,

46
00:01:56,544 --> 00:01:59,686
I get this area here where it tells me

47
00:02:00,086 --> 00:02:01,096
whether I can run

48
00:02:01,769 --> 00:02:04,735
this specific model version, this quantized

49
00:02:04,799 --> 00:02:06,787
version on my system.

50
00:02:07,460 --> 00:02:09,111
So here I got a green check mark, which

51
00:02:09,224 --> 00:02:09,736
means yes,

52
00:02:10,265 --> 00:02:12,478
this will fit into memory.

53
00:02:13,536 --> 00:02:16,084
But besides this Hugging Face feature,

54
00:02:16,662 --> 00:02:19,098
LM Studio, which is one of the main tools

55
00:02:19,162 --> 00:02:20,893
we'll use throughout this course and which

56
00:02:20,926 --> 00:02:23,298
we'll also use to download and run these

57
00:02:23,474 --> 00:02:24,827
open models on our machine,

58
00:02:25,667 --> 00:02:28,399
that tool also has a model search, which we

59
00:02:28,479 --> 00:02:31,015
will use in the next section. And there, if

60
00:02:31,015 --> 00:02:32,191
you browse models,

61
00:02:32,771 --> 00:02:35,284
you'll get a warning if it's likely too

62
00:02:35,348 --> 00:02:37,507
large for your system.

63
00:02:38,460 --> 00:02:40,039
So for example, here, this Quen 3,

64
00:02:40,667 --> 00:02:43,697
235 billion parameter model is

65
00:02:44,278 --> 00:02:46,905
likely too large for my system here.

66
00:02:48,558 --> 00:02:50,778
This one here, on the other hand, would

67
00:02:51,232 --> 00:02:51,410
work.

68
00:02:52,239 --> 00:02:54,952
So since we'll use that tool anyways in the

69
00:02:54,952 --> 00:02:57,200
next section, you can of course use that

70
00:02:57,200 --> 00:02:59,431
indication to find out which model you can

71
00:02:59,431 --> 00:03:01,133
use on your system. And if you don't have a

72
00:03:01,133 --> 00:03:03,782
lot of memory, you simply have to go for the

73
00:03:03,782 --> 00:03:05,757
versions that have less parameters.

74
00:03:06,688 --> 00:03:09,722
Of course, the model quality, the quality of

75
00:03:09,722 --> 00:03:12,564
the results produced by the model in general

76
00:03:12,692 --> 00:03:15,309
goes up as the number of parameters

77
00:03:15,422 --> 00:03:17,653
increases, but it's not a linear

78
00:03:17,653 --> 00:03:19,949
relationship. And even these super small

79
00:03:20,078 --> 00:03:22,420
models can be great for certain use cases,

80
00:03:22,789 --> 00:03:25,372
like for example, text summarization.

81
00:03:26,334 --> 00:03:28,869
You can also do the math manually, at least

82
00:03:29,045 --> 00:03:29,430
roughly.

83
00:03:30,168 --> 00:03:32,815
You can take the amount of parameters you

84
00:03:32,815 --> 00:03:36,231
have, 27 billion, for example, and you can

85
00:03:36,504 --> 00:03:39,375
forget the billion part. It's the 27 part

86
00:03:39,407 --> 00:03:40,225
that's important.

87
00:03:41,493 --> 00:03:43,546
And then if it's a 4-bit model,

88
00:03:44,364 --> 00:03:46,722
you can essentially take half of that

89
00:03:46,786 --> 00:03:50,315
parameters number here. So 13.5 in case of

90
00:03:50,315 --> 00:03:53,571
the 27 billion parameters model. Why half?

91
00:03:53,764 --> 00:03:56,268
Because a 4-bit integer takes up half a

92
00:03:56,332 --> 00:03:59,750
byte. So if you were to multiply 27 billion

93
00:04:00,023 --> 00:04:00,472
times

94
00:04:01,130 --> 00:04:04,966
half a byte, you would end up with 13.5

95
00:04:05,303 --> 00:04:07,887
gigabytes of memory required.

96
00:04:09,090 --> 00:04:10,936
Now you need to add something on top of that

97
00:04:10,936 --> 00:04:13,086
because it's not just the parameters that

98
00:04:13,086 --> 00:04:15,670
must be loaded, it's also, for example, the

99
00:04:15,670 --> 00:04:17,853
context window, so the input and output

100
00:04:17,853 --> 00:04:20,228
tokens, the chat history and so on. And

101
00:04:20,244 --> 00:04:22,330
that's why you should add something on top

102
00:04:22,330 --> 00:04:24,815
of that. That's why here we see 17

103
00:04:24,815 --> 00:04:27,412
gigabytes, for example, as a rough estimate.

104
00:04:28,502 --> 00:04:32,221
And if you then have more system and/or VRAM

105
00:04:32,414 --> 00:04:36,213
than those 17 gigabytes, you can run that

106
00:04:36,213 --> 00:04:38,665
model locally on your system. Of course,

107
00:04:38,681 --> 00:04:40,974
assuming there's nothing else on your system

108
00:04:41,086 --> 00:04:43,298
that eats up significant amounts of memory.

109
00:04:44,388 --> 00:04:46,135
It's also worth noting that the model can

110
00:04:46,135 --> 00:04:49,374
also be split between GPU and CPU and VRAM

111
00:04:49,486 --> 00:04:50,592
and regular memory.

112
00:04:51,169 --> 00:04:53,413
That's of course not as good as doing it on

113
00:04:53,413 --> 00:04:55,754
the GPU only, But it's better than just

114
00:04:55,754 --> 00:04:57,983
having a CPU and no VRAM.

115
00:04:59,058 --> 00:05:01,928
So if you have like 8 gigabytes of VRAM but

116
00:05:02,153 --> 00:05:04,943
32 gigabytes of system memory, you could

117
00:05:04,959 --> 00:05:07,348
still run this model here because 8

118
00:05:07,381 --> 00:05:09,529
gigabytes of the 17 gigabytes would be

119
00:05:09,529 --> 00:05:11,903
loaded into VRAM and the rest would be

120
00:05:11,935 --> 00:05:14,421
loaded into regular system memory. That's

121
00:05:14,501 --> 00:05:15,559
also important to note.

122
00:05:16,826 --> 00:05:18,189
And of course, if you don't have enough

123
00:05:18,205 --> 00:05:20,322
memory, you'll have to look for some other

124
00:05:20,370 --> 00:05:20,771
model.

125
00:05:21,493 --> 00:05:23,304
For example, for Google's Gemma models,

126
00:05:23,401 --> 00:05:25,132
which are really good, there also are

127
00:05:25,260 --> 00:05:28,274
smaller versions with 4 billion parameters

128
00:05:28,387 --> 00:05:29,878
instead of 27 billion

129
00:05:30,455 --> 00:05:32,940
and 1 billion and 12 billion. And of course,

130
00:05:32,956 --> 00:05:35,729
these models require way less memory in

131
00:05:35,842 --> 00:05:38,214
order to be loaded and executed.

132
00:05:38,888 --> 00:05:41,116
And that's therefore how you can answer the

133
00:05:41,116 --> 00:05:43,313
question whether it will run on your machine

134
00:05:43,313 --> 00:05:43,569
or not.

135
00:05:44,403 --> 00:05:47,289
And that's what quantization is all about

136
00:05:47,289 --> 00:05:48,491
and why it's important.
